VeridianOS: curl configure: argv is writable on Linux-like targets

configure runs a program to find out whether argv can be written to
(HAVE_WRITABLE_ARGV: curl then blanks passwords given on its command line
so that process listings do not show them). A cross build cannot run it,
so configure assumed "no" and warned "the previous check could not be made
default was used", unless the target is Apple, which it already assumes
writable. Linux targets (VeridianOS is one: an ELF process stack, as
there) have writable argv too and are now treated the same. Only the
generated configure is changed (configure.ac would make the build rerun
autoconf); the configure.ac line is:

  if test "$curl_cv_writable_argv" = "cross" && test "$curl_cv_apple" = "yes"; then

--- a/configure
+++ b/configure
@@ -25961,7 +25961,7 @@
     CC=$oldcc
   fi
 
-if test "$curl_cv_writable_argv" = "cross" && test "$curl_cv_apple" = "yes"; then
+if test "$curl_cv_writable_argv" = "cross" && { test "$curl_cv_apple" = "yes" || case $host_os in linux*) true;; *) false;; esac; }; then
   curl_cv_writable_argv=yes
 fi
 case $curl_cv_writable_argv in
